# HAL services
/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.health-service\.example    u:object_r:hal_health_default_exec:s0
/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.graphics\.allocator-service\.minigbm    u:object_r:hal_graphics_allocator_default_exec:s0

# DRM/KMS nodes. card* is privileged (SurfaceFlinger and drm_hwcomposer);
# renderD* is the unprivileged render node every app's gralloc mapper opens.
/dev/dri/card[0-9]+          u:object_r:graphics_device:s0
/dev/dri/renderD[0-9]+       u:object_r:gpu_device:s0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same-process HAL libraries.
#
# These are loaded INTO every GL client -- zygote, SurfaceFlinger, and each app
# process -- rather than being called over binder, so they have to be mappable
# and executable by all of them. Left at the default vendor_file they are not,
# and the boot fills with denials from every domain that touches graphics:
#
#     avc: denied { map }   scontext=u:r:zygote        path=".../mapper.minigbm.so"
#     avc: denied { execute} scontext=u:r:surfaceflinger path=".../libgallium_dri.so"
#
# That single mislabel accounted for 136 of the 453 denials on a boot -- by far
# the largest group. AOSP labels its own equivalents this way
# (system/sepolicy/vendor/file_contexts, gralloc.default.so and the
# graphics.mapper@N-impl libraries); these are this device's.
#
# libgallium_dri.so is Mesa's actual driver and is a direct NEEDED of
# libEGL_mesa.so, so it sits on the plain library path rather than under egl/,
# which is why the blanket egl/ rule does not cover it.
/vendor/lib(64)?/hw/mapper\.minigbm\.so      u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libminigbm_gralloc\.so      u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libgallium_dri\.so          u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0
/vendor/lib(64)?/libdrm\.so                  u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0

# SwiftShader's Vulkan driver, which ANGLE loads in-process. Same story as the
# minigbm/Mesa libraries above: at plain vendor_file even SurfaceFlinger cannot
# read it, and EGL then fails with "no suitable EGLConfig found, giving up".
/vendor/lib(64)?/hw/vulkan\.pastel\.so       u:object_r:same_process_hal_file:s0

# virtio-console ports used during bring-up: kmsg on hvc0, logcat on hvc1, and
# the screencap PNG on hvc2 (see init.pc_x86_64.rc and tools/run-qemu.sh).
# Unlabeled they fall back to the generic 'device' type, which shell may not
# write, so the log streams silently produce nothing under enforcing.
/dev/hvc[0-9]+                               u:object_r:pc_console_device:s0


# pc_select_egl.sh runs as our own early service in vendor_shell (see
# init.pc_x86_64.rc), so it lives on /vendor and is labelled vendor_shell_exec.
#
# It was init's init_dev_config hook first, labelled init_dev_config_exec. That
# is the documented slot for "per-SKU settings or sysprops" and it is simply
# never invoked on this device: the service is defined, the property points at
# this file, the file is 0755 with the right label -- and init logs no
# "starting service 'init_dev_config'" and a breadcrumb property the script
# sets comes back empty after a full boot.
/vendor/bin/pc_select_egl\.sh                u:object_r:vendor_shell_exec:s0

# init.rc's init_dev_config service is 'service init_dev_config
# ${ro.vendor.init_dev_config.path}' -- with the property unset init cannot
# expand the path, which takes ueventd and apexd-bootstrap down with it and
# reboots the device. So it still needs to point at something, and that
# something must carry init_dev_config_exec: /vendor/bin/true is toybox_vendor,
# labelled vendor_toolbox_exec, which init_dev_config may not entrypoint.
/vendor/bin/pc_noop\.sh                      u:object_r:init_dev_config_exec:s0

# The /dev/dri DIRECTORY, not just the nodes in it. Every GL client loads
# mapper.minigbm.so in-process and that enumerates the directory, so leaving it
# at the generic 'device' type denies the listing to SurfaceFlinger, bootanim,
# system_server and every app.
/dev/dri                                     u:object_r:gpu_device:s0

# Partition nodes. Without these they are the generic block_device type, and
# fsck is refused on its own filesystem:
#     avc: denied { read write } comm="tune2fs" name="nvme0n1p5"
#          scontext=u:r:fsck:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:block_device:s0
# Widening fsck is not an option -- domain.te neverallows it against
# block_device -- so the nodes have to carry their real types.
#
# The layout is fixed by tools/mkdisk.sh (1 esp, 2 system, 3 vendor,
# 4 metadata, 5 userdata); both spellings appear because the QEMU disk is NVMe
# and a USB/SATA install is sd*.
/dev/block/(nvme0n1p4|sd[a-z]4)              u:object_r:metadata_block_device:s0
/dev/block/(nvme0n1p5|sd[a-z]5)              u:object_r:userdata_block_device:s0

# Kernel-log capture destination (pc-kmsg-vendor). See sepolicy/file.te.
/data/vendor/pc(/.*)?                        u:object_r:pc_debug_data_file:s0
/vendor/bin/pc_kmsg_vendor\.sh               u:object_r:vendor_shell_exec:s0

# Mixer unmute helper. Runs in vendor_shell (see init.pc_x86_64.rc); without
# this label init cannot entrypoint it and the service never starts.
/vendor/bin/pc_audio_setup                   u:object_r:vendor_shell_exec:s0
/vendor/bin/pc_v4l2_info                     u:object_r:vendor_shell_exec:s0
